{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ad242e59b012eb3f","type":"tool_result","content":".context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/trustyai-explainability.md:14:**Short**: TrustyAI Explainability Service is a Quarkus-based Java service that provides model fairness monitoring, data drift detection, and explainability for ML models served by KServe/ModelMesh on the RHOAI platform. [source: explainability-service/src/main/resources/application.properties:102-118, explainability-service/manifests/base/trustyai-deployment.yaml:27-145]\n.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/trustyai-explainability.md:16:**Detailed**: TrustyAI Explainability Service is a Quarkus 3.8 application deployed as a Kubernetes Deployment that integrates with the KServe/ModelMesh model serving stack to provide AI model trustworthiness capabilities. It consumes inference payloads from ModelMesh via a dedicated `/consumer/kserve/v2` endpoint, storing input/output data for subsequent analysis. The service exposes REST endpoints for computing fairness metrics (Statistical Parity Difference, Disparate Impact Ratio), data drift detection (Meanshift, KS Test, Fourier MMD, Approximate KS Test), and service metadata. In the RHOAI distribution, fairness and drift endpoints are enabled while local and global explainer endpoints (SHAP, LIME, Counterfactual, TSSaliency) are disabled via Quarkus profile feature flags. The service supports optional database persistence via MariaDB/MySQL through Hibernate ORM (disabled by default), with PVC-based file storage as the primary data backend. Metrics are exposed to Prometheus at `/q/metrics` via service annotations, and the service supports TLS on port 4443 when certificates are provisioned at `/etc/tls/internal/`. [source: explainability-service/src/main/resources/application.properties:1-132, explainability-service/manifests/base/trustyai-deployment.yaml:2-185, explainability-service/src/main/java/org/kie/trustyai/service/endpoints/consumer/ConsumerEndpoint.java:29-32]\n.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/trustyai-explainability.md:24:The service has no application-level authentication mechanism. The Quarkus HTTP configuration does not include any security extension (no OIDC, no RBAC interceptor, no token validation). The OpenShift Route is configured with `tls: null`, meaning the external ingress path is plaintext. The service does configure an HTTPS listener on port 4443 with certificate files from `/etc/tls/internal/`, but this port is not exposed through the Service or Route manifests, suggesting it may be used for internal mTLS when the platform mesh is active. Authentication enforcement, if present, would need to be provided by the platform (e.g., OpenShift Service Mesh, network policies, or an external gateway). [source: explainability-service/src/main/resources/application.properties:3-7, explainability-service/manifests/base/route.yaml:17]\n.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/trustyai-explainability.md:92:| Prometheus | monitoring | metrics-target | Metrics scraping via service annotations |\n.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/trustyai-explainability.md:106:| Name | Type | Hosts | Port | Protocol | Encryption | TLS Mode | Exposure |\n.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/trustyai-explainability.md:147:This is a Java/Quarkus application running on UBI9 OpenJDK 17 runtime. FIPS compliance for Java applications on RHEL/UBI relies on the JDK using the system-level FIPS crypto providers (NSS/OpenSSL) when the host operates in FIPS mode. No explicit FIPS build flags or annotations are present.\n.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/trustyai-explainability.md:151:| **Build flags** | N/A (Java application, not Go) | Dockerfile.konflux:28 |\n.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/trustyai-explainability.md:153:| **OpenSSL in image** | Inherited from UBI9 openjdk-17-runtime base | Dockerfile.konflux:28 |\n.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/trustyai-explainability.md:155:| **TLS configuration** | Quarkus HTTP SSL on port 4443 using cert files from /etc/tls/internal/ | explainability-service/src/main/resources/application.properties:3-7 |\n.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/trustyai-explainability.md:156:| **Crypto libraries** | Java standard library crypto via OpenJDK 17 (FIPS via system providers when host is in FIPS mode) | Dockerfile.konflux:28 |\n.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/trustyai-explainability.md:157:| **Certificate handling** | Certificate files provisioned at /etc/tls/internal/tls.crt and tls.key | explainability-service/src/main/resources/application.properties:6-7 |\n.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/trustyai-explainability.md:161:- **Inference payload ingestion:** ModelMesh forwards model inference input/output payloads to TrustyAI via HTTP POST to `/consumer/kserve/v2` on port 8080. The init container configures this integration by creating a `model-serving-config` ConfigMap with the TrustyAI service URL in the current namespace. Payloads are reconciled by `ModelMeshInferencePayloadReconciler` and stored for analysis. [source: explainability-service/manifests/base/trustyai-deployment.yaml:62-81, explainability-service/src/main/java/org/kie/trustyai/service/endpoints/consumer/ConsumerEndpoint.java:29-64]\n.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/trustyai-explainability.md:162:- **Metrics computation and export:** Clients request fairness or drift metrics via REST endpoints (e.g., `/metrics/group/fairness/spd`). Scheduled metric computations are managed by `PrometheusScheduler` and exported to Prometheus at `/q/metrics`. The service is scraped by Prometheus via `prometheus.io/scrape` annotation on both the Service and Deployment. [source: explainability-service/src/main/resources/application.properties:9-11, explainability-service/src/main/java/org/kie/trustyai/service/endpoints/metrics/BaseEndpoint.java:48-49]\n.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/trustyai-explainability.md:163:- **External access:** The OpenShift Route `trustyai` provides external HTTP access to `trustyai-service` on port 80 (targeting container port 8080). TLS is not configured on the Route (`tls: null`). An internal HTTPS listener is configured on port 4443 but not exposed via the Service or Route. [source: explainability-service/manifests/base/route.yaml:1-18, explainability-service/src/main/resources/application.properties:3-7]\n.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/trustyai-explainability.md:169:- **Prometheus:** monitoring; purpose: Metrics scraping via service annotations. [source: explainability-service/manifests/base/route.yaml:1, explainability-service/manifests/base/trustyai-deployment.yaml:2, 27, 159, 177]\n.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/trustyai-explainability.md:174:| Prometheus | monitoring |  |  |  | Unknown | Metrics scraping via service annotations |\n.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/trainer.md:16:**Detailed**: The trainer component is the Kubeflow Training operator for RHOAI, implementing a controller-runtime-based Kubernetes operator that reconciles TrainJob, TrainingRuntime, and ClusterTrainingRuntime custom resources. It orchestrates distributed ML training workloads by translating high-level training specifications into JobSet resources, with pluggable runtime plugins for different training frameworks (MPI, Flux) and gang scheduling coordinators (Kubernetes Scheduler Plugins CoScheduling, Volcano). The operator runs as a single Deployment (`kubeflow-trainer-controller-manager`) exposing webhook, metrics, and status-server ports via a ClusterIP Service. It includes admission webhooks that validate and default TrainJob, TrainingRuntime, and ClusterTrainingRuntime resources. The component also ships dataset and model initializer containers as Python entrypoints that use HuggingFace Hub and OpenDAL for data retrieval, plus a Python SDK (`kubeflow_trainer_api`) for programmatic training job management. On OpenShift, the operator reads the cluster's TLS security profile from `config.openshift.io/v1/apiservers` to apply consistent cipher suite configuration across its webhook and metrics servers, gracefully falling back to hardened Intermediate defaults on non-OpenShift clusters. RHOAI-specific additions include RBAC aggregation roles (training-admin, training-edit, training-view), NetworkPolicy management per TrainJob, and training progression status monitoring. [source: cmd/trainer-controller-manager/main.go:74-176, pkg/tls/tls.go:93-144, pkg/rhai/networkpolicy.go:152-180, pkg/rhai/progression/progression.go:147, manifests/rhoai/kubeflow-training-roles.yaml:45]\n.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/trainer.md:24:The RHOAI distribution adds a progression monitoring subsystem (`pkg/rhai/progression`) that tracks training job status by inspecting Pod readiness, and a per-TrainJob NetworkPolicy management layer (`pkg/rhai/networkpolicy`) that creates network isolation boundaries around training workloads. These are RHOAI-specific extensions not present in the upstream Kubeflow project. [source: pkg/rhai/progression/progression.go:147, pkg/rhai/networkpolicy.go:152-180]\n.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/trainer.md:26:Certificate management supports both external certificate provisioning and an integrated cert-controller mode (`github.com/open-policy-agent/cert-controller`) for automatic webhook certificate rotation. The readiness probe gates on webhook server availability, ensuring the operator does not advertise readiness until admission webhooks can serve traffic. [source: cmd/trainer-controller-manager/main.go:145-158, cmd/trainer-controller-manager/main.go:216-226]\n.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/trainer.md:28:TLS configuration is centralized through `pkg/tls`, which dynamically reads the OpenShift cluster TLS security profile and applies it uniformly to metrics, webhook, and status servers. The fallback to hardened Intermediate defaults (TLS 1.2+, AEAD-only ciphers) ensures secure operation on non-OpenShift clusters. [source: pkg/tls/tls.go:93-144, cmd/trainer-controller-manager/main.go:119-128]\n.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/trainer.md:81:| /mutate-trainer-kubeflow-org-v1alpha1-trainjob | POST |  | HTTPS | Unknown | TLS | Kubernetes admission |  | Mutating admission webhook |\n.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/trainer.md:83:| /validate-trainer-kubeflow-org-v1alpha1-clustertrainingruntime | POST |  | HTTPS | Unknown | TLS | Kubernetes admission |  | Validating admission webhook |\n.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/trainer.md:84:| /validate-trainer-kubeflow-org-v1alpha1-trainingruntime | POST |  | HTTPS | Unknown | TLS | Kubernetes admission |  | Validating admission webhook |\n.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/trainer.md:85:| /validate-trainer-kubeflow-org-v1alpha1-trainjob | POST |  | HTTPS | Unknown | TLS | Kubernetes admission |  | Validating admission webhook |\n.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/trainer.md:136:| OpenShift Cluster TLS Configuration | Resource Read | runtime-configuration | Read cluster TLS security profile to configure webhook and metrics server cipher suites |\n.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/trainer.md:150:| Name | Type | Hosts | Port | Protocol | Encryption | TLS Mode | Exposure |\n.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/trainer.md:157:| Kubernetes API | 6443 | HTTPS/WSS | TLS 1.2+ | ServiceAccount | Kubernetes resource operations |\n.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/trainer.md:216:| tl