uisite). No priority collapse: E005's\nHLR-1+HLR-2 mapping is both-P0 accountability work on one artifact, not deferrable\nfeatures bundled together.\n\n### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\nTraced the full graph: no cycles; all five diagram edges (E001→E004, E001→BRANCH-A-E008,\nE002→BRANCH-A-E009, E003→E007, E004→E007) have matching frontmatter `dependencies` in\nthe target epics and vice versa — no dangling references, no extra frontmatter deps.\nArrows point dependency → dependent per convention. Edge justification is correct:\nE001→E004 is a Rule 3 true gate (rewrite, with `gated_by` and populated\n`gate_failure_impact`); E001/E002→branch epics follow the conditional-branch contract\n(branch=A, gated_by in dependencies, remove-on-failure); E003/E004→E007 are Rule 13\nartifact dependencies (the measurement consumes the rebuilt images — a measured input,\nnot a digest/URL configuration reference). Parallelism is respected: E003∥E004 (different\nimage families, no shared artifacts), and no switch→scan edges — the E006 CI scan gates\nat execution time via its pass criterion, mirroring the Rule 10/Rule 25 AC-gate pattern;\nthis is the correct call, not an unjustified omission. No konflux/repo-onboarding chains\nexist (Rule 20 correctly does not fire), so rules 5-10 pairings are vacuous. Branch\nfiles are excluded from the main-plan critical path (3), which is reasonable for a\nstrategy with two genuine external gates. Two minor observations: (a) E006's\nexception-covered classification and E005's routing entries implicitly consume the\nE001/E002 outcomes as execution-time configuration — acceptable AC-gating, but the input\nrelationship is only implicit; (b) BRANCH-A-E009 may target `rhel9-8-els/pqc-minimal` if\nthe trainer data cache turns out to be rhel9-els-based, yet lacks the AIPCC RHEL 9.8\nrebase precondition AC that E004 carries — a conditional AC would close this.\n\n### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\nEvery epic has a distinct (component, team) tuple with canonical component names from\n`.context/rhai-components.txt` (AIPCC Productization, Training Kubeflow, Build and\nRelease, AI Core Platform, CI/CD, PerfScale — all verified present). The two\nInvestigations are per-unknown, which is permitted. The family-switch epics (E003 ~68\nimages, E004 ~34, E008 ~11, E009 single image) are coordination epics owned by the\nDevOps/Build team with per-image Containerfile execution delegated to owning teams via\ntracking tickets — this matches the strategy's stated mechanism (\"DevOps/Build team:\ncoordinate base image pipeline updates\"; \"each team owns switching their image(s)\")\nrather than bundling multiple teams' work into one epic's execution. No scope\nduplication: the four switch epics apply the same change shape to disjoint image\npopulations, and E005's readiness matrix (coordination artifact) vs E006's CI report\n(automated scan output) are distinct work products for distinct teams. No\ncode-plus-content-authoring mixing. Epic sizes are within the coordination-epic frame\nthe strategy itself establishes (effort S overall).\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\nBoth Investigations genuinely change downstream structure: E001's outcome rewrites\nE004's approach (rewrite) and determines BRANCH-A-E008's existence (remove); E002's\noutcome determines BRANCH-A-E009's existence (remove). Both have downstream dependents.\nAll seven Implementations produce concrete artifacts (switched images, readiness\nmatrix, CI scan, measurement report). Gating metadata is complete and correct: E004,\nBRANCH-A-E008, and BRANCH-A-E009 each set `gated_by` to an Investigation that appears\nin their direct `dependencies`, with `gate_failure_impact.action` and\n`fallback_approach` both populated and meaningful (something concrete changes on\nfailure). E007 depends only on Implementations and correctly has no `gated_by`; no epic\nlists an Investigation dependency without `gated_by`. The E007 threshold-definition\nwork (NEEDS DOMAIN INPUT) is correctly treated as in-scope Implementation work with a\nhuman sign-off AC rather than a fake Investigation, since it does not change which\nepics exist.\n\n### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (1/2)\nSignal fields are type-appropriate everywhere: E001/E002 carry `investigation_signals`\nwith `ai_signals: null`; all seven Implementation epics (including both branch epics)\ncarry the 9-signal `ai_signals`. All nine ai-signals files exist, including both branch\nfiles, and every rationale table matches its frontmatter values. The investigation\nscoring is strong: E001's `source_accessibility: 0` / `human_judgment_required: -2`\ncorrectly capture that the gating answer is a pending external delivery commitment,\nand E002's +1/+1/0 correctly capture a source-audit answer. Two arguable values, both\non `pattern_precedent`, applied in opposite directions: E006 scores 0 while its own\nrationale cites the existing platform TLS-scanner CI (similar change, same CI\ninfrastructure — arguably +1), while E007 scores +1 citing PSASTRAT-7170, a benchmarking\noutcome document from another strategy rather than same-codebase precedent (arguably\n0). Neither contradicts the rubric outright, but the pair shows inconsistent\napplication of the same signal. Minor.\n\n### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\nEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the strategy — the switch epics use the\nstrategy's own verifiable commands (`update-crypto-policies --show` → `DEFAULT:PQ`,\n`openssl list -kem-algorithms` → ML-KEM-512/768/1024), the exception AC replicates the\nstrategy's zero-unaccounted-images criterion with a date, and E007's ACs are concrete\n(threshold defined and signed off, measurements with baselines, go/no-go before ship).\nRule-mandated ACs are present on every epic that meets the rule's criteria, not just\nthe first: all four replacing switch epics (E003, E004, BRANCH-A-E008, BRANCH-A-E009)\ncarry the Rule 23 rollback AC backed by the opportunistic-hybrid-key-exchange\nrationale, and all four also carry build-pipeline-green ACs (Rule 25 spirit, noted in\nthe summary). No docs-authoring epic exists, so Rule 24 is vacuous. No epic body\nreferences sibling epics by draft ID — verified by scanning all bodies; the only epic-ID\nstrings are frontmatter `dependencies`/`gated_by`, and E007 describes its dependencies\nin plain terms (\"the ubi9-minimal family switch and the rhel9-6-els family switch\").\n\n### 7. Completeness (1/2)\nCore scope is fully accounted for: every base-image family from the strategy's mapping\ntable has a switch owner (ubi9-minimal → E003, rhel9-6-els → E004, python/nodejs →\nBRANCH-A-E008, trainer data cache → BRANCH-A-E009), the known non-OpenSSL components\n(TrustyAI Java/NSS, fms-guardrails-orchestrator rustls+ring) route through E005's\nexception path per the strategy, and both conditional-branch outcomes (variants\navailable / not available; OpenSSL-backed / pure-Rust) are covered — outcome B is fully\naccounted for by the main-plan E005 exception path in each case. All three strategy\nacceptance criteria map (AC-1 → E006 + switch epics, AC-2/AC-3 → E005). Strategy-level\ncontext is carried, not dropped: FIPS-mode caveat (E006 reporting, E005 assumptions),\nbackwards compatibility (rollback ACs), rebase-slip and variant risks (E004 gate and\nE008 gate-failure impact), the \"to be created\" readiness matrix design doc (E005), and\nthe 3.5-CSV vs 3.6-payload inventory discrepancy (explicit E005 AC). Documentation\nSupport is \"Pending human review\", not Yes, so the Rule 11 docs-authoring requirement\ndoes not fire — correctly flagged as a health warning instead. One minor scope gap:\nE007's TLS-handshake performance validation is explicitly limited to the two main-plan\nimage families, and BRANCH-A-E008 (~11 images) and BRANCH-A-E009 carry no\nlatency-threshold AC even though E003 and E004 each do — the strategy's Performance NFR\n(\"TLS handshake latency regression measurement needed before ship\") is not\nfamily-limited, so branch-switched images would ship without perf validation coverage.\nA main-plan epic cannot depend on branch epics (correctly avoided), so the fix is a\nlatency AC on the branch epics themselves.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":56159,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bf0a326a4c4a3969","agent_id":"acc1233cc06bd6f35","session_id":"ca054b9d-00ed-4360-a728-320abe6368d2","uuid":"5b05ca93-51b3-4743-8334-44f97e840c3d","timestamp":"2026-10-22T10:46:44.011Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8001 decomposition"}
