{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-81c174fcef0a2d7b","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8001\n3\tscore: 12\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: minor\n8\t  criterion: AI Implementability Scoring\n9\t  description: 'pattern_precedent applied inconsistently: E006 scores 0 while its\n10\t    rationale cites the existing platform TLS-scanner CI (similar change in the same\n11\t    CI infrastructure, arguably +1), while E007 scores +1 citing PSASTRAT-7170, an\n12\t    outcome document from another strategy rather than same-codebase precedent (arguably\n13\t    0). Both values are arguable rather than rubric-contradicting.'\n14\t- severity: minor\n15\t  criterion: Completeness\n16\t  description: E007 limits TLS-handshake performance validation to the ubi9-minimal\n17\t    and rhel9-6-els families; BRANCH-A-E008 (~11 images) and BRANCH-A-E009 carry no\n18\t    latency-threshold AC even though E003/E004 each do. The strategy Performance NFR\n19\t    (measurement needed before ship) is not family-limited — add a latency AC to the\n20\t    branch switch epics.\n21\t- severity: minor\n22\t  criterion: DAG Coherence\n23\t  description: E006 exception-covered classification and E005 routing entries implicitly\n24\t    consume E001/E002 outcomes as execution-time configuration with no explicit input\n25\t    AC or note linking them; acceptable AC-gating per the Rule 10 philosophy, but\n26\t    the input relationship is implicit.\n27\t- severity: minor\n28\t  criterion: DAG Coherence\n29\t  description: BRANCH-A-E009 may target rhel9-8-els/pqc-minimal if the trainer data\n30\t    cache is rhel9-els-based, yet lacks the AIPCC RHEL 9.8 rebase precondition AC\n31\t    that E004 carries; add a conditional rebase AC to close the gap.\n32\terror: null\n33\t---\n34\t## Review Summary\n35\t\n36\tScore: 12/14 — pass\n37\tRecommendation: accept\n38\t\n39\tFull decomposition (triage: null, epic_count 7 + 2 conditional branch epics) — the\n40\tmulti-epic structure is expected for a non-triaged strategy, so no triage contradiction.\n41\tThe DAG is acyclic, diagram arrows run dependency → dependent and match every epic's\n42\tfrontmatter `dependencies` exactly (E004 and E007 carry the five diagram edges; E001,\n43\tE002, E003, E005, E006 are roots). Both branch files set `branch: A`, `gated_by`, and\n44\t`gate_failure_impact` with the gate present in `dependencies`, and no main-plan epic\n45\tlists a branch epic. No critical or major issues found; four minor issues.\n46\t\n47\t## Criterion Details\n48\t\n49\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n50\tAll three P0 HLRs are mapped and verified against epic \"HLR Traceability\" sections:\n51\tHLR-1 (PQC base image) → E003/E004/BRANCH-A-E008/BRANCH-A-E009 with E005 accountability\n52\tand E006 automated verification; HLR-2 (OCP exceptions) → E005, correctly treating\n53\tper-team filing as out-of-scope per the strategy while enforcing/aggregating it; HLR-3\n54\t(runtime-only, final-stage-only) → carried as an explicit AC on every switch epic. The\n55\tDeferred ML-DSA HLR is explicitly deferred in the traceability matrix — no coverage\n56\trequired. Priority inheritance is sound: E001/E002 (P0) gate P0 downstream work; E007\n57\tis P1 as an NFR-derived epic whose prerequisites (E003/E004) are P0 — no inversion\n58\t(prerequisites are higher priority than the work they enable), and the decomposer\n59\tdocumented the P1 rationale. E007 mapping to no HLR is acceptable and explicitly\n60\texplained (NFR + Performance Team Support prerequisite). No priority collapse: E005's\n61\tHLR-1+HLR-2 mapping is both-P0 accountability work on one artifact, not deferrable\n62\tfeatures bundled together.\n63\t\n64\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n65\tTraced the full graph: no cycles; all five diagram edges (E001→E004, E001→BRANCH-A-E008,\n66\tE002→BRANCH-A-E009, E003→E007, E004→E007) have matching frontmatter `dependencies` in\n67\tthe target epics and vice versa — no dangling references, no extra frontmatter deps.\n68\tArrows point dependency → dependent per convention. Edge justification is correct:\n69\tE001→E004 is a Rule 3 true gate (rewrite, with `gated_by` and populated\n70\t`gate_failure_impact`); E001/E002→branch epics follow the conditional-branch contract\n71\t(branch=A, gated_by in dependencies, remove-on-failure); E003/E004→E007 are Rule 13\n72\tartifact dependencies (the measurement consumes the rebuilt images — a measured input,\n73\tnot a digest/URL configuration reference). Parallelism is respected: E003∥E004 (different\n74\timage families, no shared artifacts), and no switch→scan edges — the E006 CI scan gates\n75\tat execution time via its pass criterion, mirroring the Rule 10/Rule 25 AC-gate pattern;\n76\tthis is the correct call, not an unjustified omission. No konflux/repo-onboarding chains\n77\texist (Rule 20 correctly does not fire), so rules 5-10 pairings are vacuous. Branch\n78\tfiles are excluded from the main-plan critical path (3), which is reasonable for a\n79\tstrategy with two genuine external gates. Two minor observations: (a) E006's\n80\texception-covered classification and E005's routing entries implicitly consume the\n81\tE001/E002 outcomes as execution-time configuration — acceptable AC-gating, but the input\n82\trelationship is only implicit; (b) BRANCH-A-E009 may target `rhel9-8-els/pqc-minimal` if\n83\tthe trainer data cache turns out to be rhel9-els-based, yet lacks the AIPCC RHEL 9.8\n84\trebase precondition AC that E004 carries — a conditional AC would close this.\n85\t\n86\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n87\tEvery epic has a distinct (component, team) tuple with canonical component names from\n88\t`.context/rhai-components.txt` (AIPCC Productization, Training Kubeflow, Build and\n89\tRelease, AI Core Platform, CI/CD, PerfScale — all verified present). The two\n90\tInvestigations are per-unknown, which is permitted. The family-switch epics (E003 ~68\n91\timages, E004 ~34, E008 ~11, E009 single image) are coordination epics owned by the\n92\tDevOps/Build team with per-image Containerfile execution delegated to owning teams via\n93\ttracking tickets — this matches the strategy's stated mechanism (\"DevOps/Build team:\n94\tcoordinate base image pipeline updates\"; \"each team owns switching their image(s)\")\n95\trather than bundling multiple teams' work into one epic's execution. No scope\n96\tduplication: the four switch epics apply the same change shape to disjoint image\n97\tpopulations, and E005's readiness matrix (coordination artifact) vs E006's CI report\n98\t(automated scan output) are distinct work products for distinct teams. No\n99\tcode-plus-content-authoring mixing. Epic sizes are within the coordination-epic frame\n100\tthe strategy itself establishes (effort S overall).\n101\t\n102\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n103\tBoth Investigations genuinely change downstream structure: E001's outcome rewrites\n104\tE004's approach (rewrite) and determines BRANCH-A-E008's existence (remove); E002's\n105\toutcome determines BRANCH-A-E009's existence (remove). Both have downstream dependents.\n106\tAll seven Implementations produce concrete artifacts (switched images, readiness\n107\tmatrix, CI scan, measurement report). Gating metadata is complete and correct: E004,\n108\tBRANCH-A-E008, and BRANCH-A-E009 each set `gated_by` to an Investigation that appears\n109\tin their direct `dependencies`, with `gate_failure_impact.action` and\n110\t`fallback_approach` both populated and meaningful (something concrete changes on\n111\tfailure). E007 depends only on Implementations and correctly has no `gated_by`; no epic\n112\tlists an Investigation dependency without `gated_by`. The E007 threshold-definition\n113\twork (NEEDS DOMAIN INPUT) is correctly treated as in-scope Implementation work with a\n114\thuman sign-off AC rather than a fake Investigation, since it does not change which\n115\tepics exist.\n116\t\n117\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (1/2)\n118\tSignal fields are type-appropriate everywhere: E001/E002 carry `investigation_signals`\n119\twith `ai_signals: null`; all seven Implementation epics (including both branch epics)\n120\tcarry the 9-signal `ai_signals`. All nine ai-signals files exist, including both branch\n121\tfiles, and every rationale table matches its frontmatter values. The investigation\n122\tscoring is strong: E001's `source_accessibility: 0` / `human_judgment_required: -2`\n123\tcorrectly capture that the gating answer is a pending external delivery commitment,\n124\tand E002's +1/+1/0 correctly capture a source-audit answer. Two arguable values, both\n125\ton `pattern_precedent`, applied in opposite directions: E006 scores 0 while its own\n126\trationale cites the existing platform TLS-scanner CI (similar change, same CI\n127\tinfrastructure — arguably +1), while E007 scores +1 citing PSASTRAT-7170, a benchmarking\n128\toutcome document from another strategy rather than same-codebase precedent (arguably\n129\t0). Neither contradicts the rubric outright, but the pair shows inconsistent\n130\tapplication of the same signal. Minor.\n131\t\n132\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n133\tEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the strategy — the switch epics use the\n134\tstrategy's own verifiable commands (`update-crypto-policies --show` → `DEFAULT:PQ`,\n135\t`openssl list -kem-algorithms` → ML-KEM-512/768/1024), the exception AC replicates the\n136\tstrategy's zero-unaccounted-images criterion with a date, and E007's ACs are concrete\n137\t(threshold defined and signed off, measurements with baselines, go/no-go before ship).\n138\tRule-mandated ACs are present on every epic that meets the rule's criteria, not just\n139\tthe first: all four replacing switch epics (E003, E004, BRANCH-A-E008, BRANCH-A-E009)\n140\tcarry the Rule 23 rollback AC backed by the opportunistic-hybrid-key-exchange\n141\trationale, and all four also carry build-pipeline-green ACs (Rule 25 spirit, noted in\n142\tthe summary). No docs-authoring epic exists, so Rule 24 is vacuous. No epic body\n143\treferences sibling epics by draft ID — verified by scanning all bodies; the only epic-ID\n144\tstrings are frontmatter `dependencies`/`gated_by`, and E007 describes its dependencies\n145\tin plain terms (\"the ubi9-minimal family switch and the rhel9-6-els family switch\").\n146\t\n147\t### 7. Completeness (1/2)\n148\tCore scope is fully accounted for: every base-image family from the strategy's mapping\n149\ttable has a switch owner (ubi9-minimal → E003, rhel9-6-els → E004, python/nodejs →\n150\tBRANCH-A-E008, trainer data cache → BRANCH-A-E009), the known non-OpenSSL components\n151\t(TrustyAI Java/NSS, fms-guardrails-orchestrator rustls+ring) route through E005's\n152\texception path per the strategy, and both conditional-branch outcomes (variants\n153\tavailable / not available; OpenSSL-backed / pure-Rust) are covered — outcome B is fully\n154\taccounted for by the main-plan E005 exception path in each case. All three strategy\n155\tacceptance criteria map (AC-1 → E006 + switch epics, AC-2/AC-3 → E005). Strategy-level\n156\tcontext is carried, not dropped: FIPS-mode caveat (E006 reporting, E005 assumptions),\n157\tbackwards compatibility (rollback ACs), rebase-slip and variant risks (E004 gate and\n158\tE008 gate-failure impact), the \"to be created\" readiness matrix design doc (E005), and\n159\tthe 3.5-CSV vs 3.6-payload inventory discrepancy (explicit E005 AC). Documentation\n160\tSupport is \"Pending human review\", not Yes, so the Rule 11 docs-authoring requirement\n161\tdoes not fire — correctly flagged as a health warning instead. One minor scope gap:\n162\tE007's TLS-handshake performance validation is explicitly limited to the two main-plan\n163\timage families, and BRANCH-A-E008 (~11 images) and BRANCH-A-E009 carry no\n164\tlatency-threshold AC even though E003 and E004 each do — the strategy's Performance NFR\n165\t(\"TLS handshake latency regression measurement needed before ship\") is not\n166\tfamily-limited, so branch-switched images would ship without perf validation coverage.\n167\tA main-plan epic cannot depend on branch epics (correctly avoided), so the fix is a\n168\tlatency AC on the branch epics themselves.\n169\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b6679c9c492244df","agent_id":"ade7f978058bfdf0c","session_id":"ca054b9d-00ed-4360-a728-320abe6368d2","uuid":"221e8b13-23c9-4fee-8405-4c52943efada","timestamp":"2026-10-22T10:47:11.455Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-81c174fcef0a2d7b","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8001 decomposition"}
