e image pipeline updates\".\n96\t2. **Performance NFR lacks a numeric threshold** — flagged NEEDS DOMAIN INPUT in the strategy\n97\t   (owner: PM + Performance team lead, due before Sprint 2 ends). Resolution does not change epic\n98\t   structure, so it is carried as scope/AC on E007, not an Investigation. Reference points:\n99\t   PSASTRAT-7170 benchmarking outcome; Chrome ~10% max TLS handshake regression.\n100\t3. **NFR-1 implies TLS-stack migration work for pure-Rust components** (fms-guardrails-orchestrator,\n101\t   rustls+ring) that no epic in this set implements — the strategy's chosen path is an OCP exception\n102\t   with a remediation plan (migration spike RHOAIENG-7452 is referenced but not scoped here). The\n103\t   ML-KEM-after-migration requirement is carried as an AC on E005's exception-validation criteria.\n104\t   Flag for PM validation.\n105\t4. **CI scan ownership boundary:** AC-1 is \"measured by automated CI scan\", while TLS-scanner CI is\n106\t   owned by the separate Central TLS Profile strategy (RHAISTRAT-7816). E006 is scoped strictly to\n107\t   crypto-policy / ML-KEM algorithm verification, not TLS adherence scanning. Boundary to confirm\n108\t   with the owning strategy.\n109\t5. **Strategy-level process gates pending human review** (Architecture Review, ODH/RHOAI Build\n110\t   Onboarding, Licence Validation, Documentation Support). Documentation Support is \"Pending human\n111\t   review\", not Yes — no `docs-authoring` epic generated. If it flips to Yes, a docs-authoring epic\n112\t   must be added (Rule 11).\n113\t6. **Inventory discrepancy:** the strategy counts 153 relatedImages in the RHOAI 3.5 build-config\n114\t   CSV while the 3.6-ea.1 architecture context analyzes 72 components. E005 must reconcile the 3.5\n115\t   CSV inventory against the 3.6 payload; discrepancies documented, not silently dropped.\n116\t7. **Priority note (not an inversion):** E007 is P1 — it derives from an NFR (\"required before\n117\t   ship\"), not from an HLR; all HLR-mapped epics are P0.\n118\t8. **External timing risks carried forward:** AIPCC RHEL 9.8 rebase slip and PQC Python/Node.js\n119\t   variant non-availability both have defined fallbacks (OCP exceptions) via tiered delivery below.\n120\t\n121\t## Tiered Delivery\n122\t\n123\tExternal dependency with uncertain timing (AIPCC base image delivery) — partial value is\n124\tdeliverable without it, so the strategy splits:\n125\t\n126\t- **Tier 1 (independent, start immediately):** E001, E002, E003 (~68 images on the confirmed\n127\t  `ubi9-minimal-pqc` base), E005 (readiness matrix + compliance coordination), E006 (CI scan).\n128\t- **Tier 2 (dependency-gated):** E004 (~34 images, gated by AIPCC RHEL 9.8 rebase / E001 outcome),\n129\t  BRANCH-A-E008 (~11 images, gated by PQC variant availability), BRANCH-A-E009 (single image,\n130\t  gated by the TLS audit), E007 (measures switched images from both family switches).\n131\t\n132\t## Conditional Branches\n133\t\n134\t- **Gate E001 (AIPCC PQC base availability), outcome A — variants available with committed delivery\n135\t  before September 15, 2026:** `BRANCH-A-E008` switches the Python/Node.js-based images\n136\t  (5× python-311 training variants + lmes-job, 4× python-312 training variants, 1×\n137\t  python-312-minimal feature-server, 1× nodejs-22 dashboard). `gate_failure_impact: remove` —\n138\t  if the variants are not available, this epic does not exist and those images file OCP exceptions\n139\t  through the main-plan compliance coordination epic.\n140\t- **Gate E001, outcome B — variants not available in time:** no branch epics; affected images route\n141\t  to the OCP exception path covered by E005 (HLR-2). This outcome is fully accounted for by the\n142\t  main plan.\n143\t- **Gate E002 (trainer data cache TLS audit), outcome A — OpenSSL-backed (native-tls) stack:**\n144\t  `BRANCH-A-E009` switches the trainer data cache image to the appropriate PQC base for its family.\n145\t  `gate_failure_impact