{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a92766c80eae03b0","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8001\n3\tepic_count: 7\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8001 (Base image compliance — Post-quantum cryptography support)\n11\t\n12\tStrategy is a PQC base-image compliance coordination tracker for RHOAI 3.6 / OCP 5.0 with a\n13\tSeptember 15, 2026 deadline. It is NOT below-threshold: although effort-rated S, it spans all\n14\tRHOAI component images (multiple components), all RHOAI component teams plus the AIPCC and\n15\tDevOps/Build teams, and contains genuine unknowns (PQC Python/Node.js base variants, RHEL 9.8\n16\trebase date, trainer data cache TLS posture) whose answers change which downstream epics exist.\n17\tIt is not documentation-only: the scope requires Containerfile final-stage changes and CI work.\n18\tFull decomposition follows.\n19\t\n20\tStaff Engineer / SME Input: section contains only template placeholder text — no overrides to apply.\n21\t\n22\t## Epic List\n23\t\n24\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n25\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n26\t| E001 | Confirm AIPCC PQC base image availability (Python/Node.js variants and RHEL 9.8 rebase date) | Investigation | AIPCC team | P0 |\n27\t| E002 | Audit trainer data cache TLS stack (OpenSSL-backed vs pure-Rust) | Investigation | Training Kubeflow | P0 |\n28\t| E003 | Switch ubi9-minimal-based platform images to ubi9-minimal-pqc | Implementation | DevOps/Build team | P0 |\n29\t| E004 | Switch rhel9-6-els-based images to rhel9-8-els/pqc-minimal | Implementation | DevOps/Build team | P0 |\n30\t| E005 | PQC readiness matrix and compliance coordination | Implementation | AI Core Platform | P0 |\n31\t| E006 | Automated PQC compliance CI scan for the RHOAI payload | Implementation | CI/CD | P0 |\n32\t| E007 | TLS handshake performance validation for PQC-switched images | Implementation | PerfScale | P1 |\n33\t| BRANCH-A-E008 | Switch Python/Node.js-based images to confirmed PQC base variants | Implementation (branch A of E001) | DevOps/Build team | P0 |\n34\t| BRANCH-A-E009 | Switch trainer data cache image to a PQC base | Implementation (branch A of E002) | Training Kubeflow | P0 |\n35\t\n36\tMain plan: 7 epics (E001–E007) + 2 conditional branch epics. `epic_count` counts main-plan epics.\n37\t\n38\t## Dependency DAG\n39\t\n40\t```mermaid\n41\tgraph TD\n42\t  E001[\"E001 Investigation: AIPCC PQC base availability\"]\n43\t  E002[\"E002 Investigation: trainer data cache TLS audit\"]\n44\t  E003[\"E003 Implementation: ubi9-minimal family switch\"]\n45\t  E004[\"E004 Implementation: rhel9-6-els family switch\"]\n46\t  E005[\"E005 Implementation: readiness matrix + compliance coordination\"]\n47\t  E006[\"E006 Implementation: PQC compliance CI scan\"]\n48\t  E007[\"E007 Implementation: TLS handshake performance validation\"]\n49\t  BRANCH_A_E008[\"BRANCH-A-E008 (conditional: variants available)\"]\n50\t  BRANCH_A_E009[\"BRANCH-A-E009 (conditional: OpenSSL-backed TLS)\"]\n51\t\n52\t  E001 --> E004\n53\t  E001 --> BRANCH_A_E008\n54\t  E002 --> BRANCH_A_E009\n55\t  E003 --> E007\n56\t  E004 --> E007\n57\t```\n58\t\n59\tRoots (no dependencies, start immediately — Tier 1): E001, E002, E003, E005, E006.\n60\tMain-plan critical path: E001 → E004 → E007 (length 3).\n61\t\n62\t## DAG Justification\n63\t\n64\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n65\t|------|------|-----------|\n66\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 3 (Investigation determines downstream scope/approach) | The AIPCC RHEL 9.8 rebase committed date (AIPCC-7258) determines whether the ~34 rhel9-6-els-based images can adopt `rhel9-8-els/pqc-minimal` by September 15 or must pivot to OCP exceptions. True gate: `gated_by=E001`, action `rewrite`. |\n67\t| E001 → BRANCH-A-E008 | Rule 3 + conditional branch (bounded outcome, 2 possibilities) | Whether PQC variants of python-311/312/python-312-minimal/nodejs-22 exist with committed delivery before September 15 decides whether a switch epic for those ~11 images exists at all. Outcome A → branch epic; outcome B → no branch, images route to the OCP-exception path already covered by E005. |\n68\t| E002 → BRANCH-A-E009 | Rule 3 + conditional branch (bounded outcome, 2 possibilities) | The trainer data cache TLS audit decides whether a base swap remediates that image (OpenSSL-backed) or an OCP exception with TLS-migration remediation plan is required (pure rustls). Outcome A → branch epic; outcome B → no branch, exception path via E005. |\n69\t| E003 → E007 | Rule 13 (artifact dependency) | Performance validation measures the rebuilt PQC-switched images; the measurement work builds on the switched image artifact produced by the family switch. |\n70\t| E004 → E007 | Rule 13 (artifact dependency) | Same: rhel9-els family switched images are a measured input to the performance validation. |\n71\t| No edge E003 ↔ E004 | Rules 14/15 (parallel) | Different image families, different target bases, no shared artifacts — Tier 1 vs Tier 2 split (Step 5.5). |\n72\t| No edge E003/E004 → E006 | Rule 10 philosophy (AC-gated, not DAG-coupled) | The CI scan is built in parallel; its \"all OpenSSL-backed images report DEFAULT:PQ\" pass criterion gates at execution time against whatever payload exists, exactly like the konflux \"build pipeline green\" AC pattern. Wiring switch → scan edges would serialize naturally-parallel work. |\n73\t| No edge E001/E002 → E005 | Rule 4 boundary | The readiness matrix records pending outcomes and fills routing entries dynamically; coordination does not block on the investigations. |\n74\t| No edge for AIPCC-7258 itself | Rule 21 (third party resolves → precondition, no epic) | The RHEL 9.8 rebase is executed by AIPCC under AIPCC-7258, outside this strategy's epic set; it is captured as a precondition AC on E004 plus the E001 gate. |\n75\t\n76\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n77\t\n78\t| HLR | Priority | Epics | Notes |\n79\t|-----|----------|-------|-------|\n80\t| All RHOAI runtime images must use a PQC-enabled base image for RHOAI 3.6 / OCP 5.0 | P0 | E003, E004, BRANCH-A-E008, BRANCH-A-E009 (switch execution); E005 (accountability: every image switched or exception-filed, zero unaccounted); E006 (automated verification of AC-1) | Images that cannot switch are covered through the HLR-2 exception alternative (TrustyAI Java/NSS, fms-guardrails-orchestrator rustls+ring, trainer data cache if pure-Rust, Python/Node.js images if variants unavailable). |\n81\t| Teams unable to comply by September 15 must file an OCP exception with a remediation plan | P0 | E005 | Filing itself is each team's own work (explicitly out of scope per strategy); E005 enforces and aggregates. |\n82\t| PQC enablement is runtime only — only the final stage changes, no builder image change | P0 | E003, E004, BRANCH-A-E008, BRANCH-A-E009 (scope constraint + AC) | Constraint carried as AC on every switch epic. |\n83\t| PQC-signed X.509 certificates (ML-DSA) | Deferred | — | Explicitly deferred by the strategy; no epic, no coverage required. |\n84\t\n85\tStrategy acceptance criteria coverage: AC-1 (crypto policy verification) → E006 (+ rebuild work in\n86\tswitch epics); AC-2 (zero unaccounted images by September 15) → E005; AC-3 (tracking tickets\n87\tlinked, aggregated view) → E005.\n88\t\n89\t## Health Warnings\n90\t\n91\t1. **Missing structured tables (non-blocking):** Affected Components is prose (\"all RHOAI component\n92\t   images\") and Impacted Teams is a bullet list, not tables. Decomposition follows the strategy's\n93\t   own base-image family mapping. Per-image Containerfile execution is delegated to owning\n94\t   component teams via tracking tickets linked to this strategy (the strategy's stated mechanism);\n95\t   the family switch epics are coordination epics owned by the DevOps/Build team, whose stated\n96\t   involvement is \"coordinate base image pipeline updates\".\n97\t2. **Performance NFR lacks a numeric threshold** — flagged NEEDS DOMAIN INPUT in the strategy\n98\t   (owner: PM + Performance team lead, due before Sprint 2 ends). Resolution does not change epic\n99\t   structure, so it is carried as scope/AC on E007, not an Investigation. Reference points:\n100\t   PSASTRAT-7170 benchmarking outcome; Chrome ~10% max TLS handshake regression.\n101\t3. **NFR-1 implies TLS-stack migration work for pure-Rust components** (fms-guardrails-orchestrator,\n102\t   rustls+ring) that no epic in this set implements — the strategy's chosen path is an OCP exception\n103\t   with a remediation plan (migration spike RHOAIENG-7452 is referenced but not scoped here). The\n104\t   ML-KEM-after-migration requirement is carried as an AC on E005's exception-validation criteria.\n105\t   Flag for PM validation.\n106\t4. **CI scan ownership boundary:** AC-1 is \"measured by automated CI scan\", while TLS-scanner CI is\n107\t   owned by the separate Central TLS Profile strategy (RHAISTRAT-7816). E006 is scoped strictly to\n108\t   crypto-policy / ML-KEM algorithm verification, not TLS adherence scanning. Boundary to confirm\n109\t   with the owning strategy.\n110\t5. **Strategy-level process gates pending human review** (Architecture Review, ODH/RHOAI Build\n111\t   Onboarding, Licence Validation, Documentation Support). Documentation Support is \"Pending human\n112\t   review\", not Yes — no `docs-authoring` epic generated. If it flips to Yes, a docs-authoring epic\n113\t   must be added (Rule 11).\n114\t6. **Inventory discrepancy:** the strategy counts 153 relatedImages in the RHOAI 3.5 build-config\n115\t   CSV while the 3.6-ea.1 architecture context analyzes 72 components. E005 must reconcile the 3.5\n116\t   CSV inventory against the 3.6 payload; discrepancies documented, not silently dropped.\n117\t7. **Priority note (not an inversion):** E007 is P1 — it derives from an NFR (\"required before\n118\t   ship\"), not from an HLR; all HLR-mapped epics are P0.\n119\t8. **External timing risks carried forward:** AIPCC RHEL 9.8 rebase slip and PQC Python/Node.js\n120\t   variant non-availability both have defined fallbacks (OCP exceptions) via tiered delivery below.\n121\t\n122\t## Tiered Delivery\n123\t\n124\tExternal dependency with uncertain timing (AIPCC base image delivery) — partial value is\n125\tdeliverable without it, so the strategy splits:\n126\t\n127\t- **Tier 1 (independent, start immediately):** E001, E002, E003 (~68 images on the confirmed\n128\t  `ubi9-minimal-pqc` base), E005 (readiness matrix + compliance coordination), E006 (CI scan).\n129\t- **Tier 2 (dependency-gated):** E004 (~34 images, gated by AIPCC RHEL 9.8 rebase / E001 outcome),\n130\t  BRANCH-A-E008 (~11 images, gated by PQC variant availability), BRANCH-A-E009 (single image,\n131\t  gated by the TLS audit), E007 (measures switched images from both family switches).\n132\t\n133\t## Conditional Branches\n134\t\n135\t- **Gate E001 (AIPCC PQC base availability), outcome A — variants available with committed delivery\n136\t  before September 15, 2026:** `BRANCH-A-E008` switches the Python/Node.js-based images\n137\t  (5× python-311 training variants + lmes-job, 4× python-312 training variants, 1×\n138\t  python-312-minimal feature-server, 1× nodejs-22 dashboard). `gate_failure_impact: remove` —\n139\t  if the variants are not available, this epic does not exist and those images file OCP exceptions\n140\t  through the main-plan compliance coordination epic.\n141\t- **Gate E001, outcome B — variants not available in time:** no branch epics; affected images route\n142\t  to the OCP exception path covered by E005 (HLR-2). This outcome is fully accounted for by the\n143\t  main plan.\n144\t- **Gate E002 (trainer data cache TLS audit), outcome A — OpenSSL-backed (native-tls) stack:**\n145\t  `BRANCH-A-E009` switches the trainer data cache image to the appropriate PQC base for its family.\n146\t  `gate_failure_impact: remove` — if the stack is pure rustls+ring, a base swap cannot remediate it\n147\t  and the owning team files an OCP exception with a TLS-migration remediation plan (covered by E005).\n148\t- **Gate E002, outcome B — pure-Rust TLS stack:** no branch epics; exception path via E005.\n149\t\n150\tBranch epics are excluded from the main-plan critical path; no main-plan epic lists a branch epic\n151\tin its dependencies.\n152\t\n153\t## Component Mapping Log\n154\t\n155\tCanonical names from `.context/rhai-components.txt`; non-obvious mappings:\n156\t\n157\t| Strategy term | Canonical component | Rationale |\n158\t|---------------|--------------------|-----------|\n159\t| AIPCC team / PQC python-nodejs base image delivery | AIPCC Productization | AIPCC owns base image productization; strategy names the \"AIPCC team\". |\n160\t| trainer data cache | Training Kubeflow | Closest canonical component for the trainer component family; the data cache image belongs to the trainer stack. |\n161\t| Family-wide base image switch coordination | Build and Release | DevOps/Build team (Impacted Teams: \"coordinate base image pipeline updates\") owns RHOAI-Build-Config and build pipeline coordination. |\n162\t| PQC readiness matrix / compliance coordination | AI Core Platform | Platform-wide strategy ownership; no single component team owns the aggregate view. |\n163\t| Automated compliance CI scan | CI/CD | CI verification work. |\n164\t| TLS handshake performance validation | PerfScale | Performance team support is Required per strategy prerequisites. |\n165\t\n166\t## Notes\n167\t\n168\t- Rule 19 (safety-critical strategy): does not fire — this is a crypto-policy compliance switch,\n169\t  not guardrails/sandboxing/RBAC work. Backwards compatibility is confirmed (X25519MLKEM768 hybrid\n170\t  key exchange negotiates opportunistically; non-PQC clients continue via classic ECDHE), and\n171\t  rollback ACs are added to all replacing switch epics per Rule 23.\n172\t- Rule 20 (new component onboarding): does not fire — all affected components already ship in the\n173\t  platform; no repo/konflux onboarding chains are needed. Build-pipeline-green ACs are still added\n174\t  to switch epics (Rule 25 spirit).\n175\t- FIPS + PQC are incompatible on UBI9: on FIPS-mode clusters DEFAULT:PQ is expected non-functional\n176\t  and is not a compliance failure for RHOAI 3.6 (confirmed assumption; carried into E006's\n177\t  reporting expectations).\n178\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-92622886643e7d18","agent_id":"a1f7a18ed15aee595","session_id":"ca054b9d-00ed-4360-a728-320abe6368d2","uuid":"7130d046-306d-483e-bfc3-e50eadc4ef97","timestamp":"2026-10-22T10:50:35.855Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a92766c80eae03b0","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8001"}
